A||||10||Cooter||Engine/ecm wiring question
Z||000000||Cooter||07-02-99||10:27 PM||||I was thinking about putting the v6/automatic from my mom's wrecked Cavalier z24 into one of my "dead" 4 cyl. cars. I would use the ecm harness from the Cavalier, but where the engine harness plugs into the ecm there is a second smaller harness that runs (on the Fiero) forward-(maybe to the instruments??) The fiero harness has fewer wires in the smaller harness than the Cavalier, but the plugs are the same. Does anybody know what the function of this set of wires would be???<p>I think this would be a good swap because the z24 made use of a more advanced ecm system than the fiero used. The z24 has aluminum heads, sequential efi, dis ignition and the all-mighty Mass AirFlow system. Before the z24 got wrecked (uninsured motorest that lied about info on the wreck report!!!) it was a HOSS! I believe this engine in the plain 85 sport coupe would be a "sleeper" and make those pesky kids think twice before messing with another Fiero!
Z||000001||batboy||07-02-99||10:59 PM||||I believe the Z-24's used those HO splayed valve heads and are supposed to run pretty good. You might have to use the entire computer and all the electronics off of the Z-28 too, I'm not sure.
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||07-02-99||11:01 PM||||Cooter, I just spent all day up to my knees in those wires. The smaller plug on the passenger side of the Fiero goes to:<br>-instruments<br>-safty switch on the clutch pedal<br>-stereo,speaker wires <br>-windows if equipped<br>-all the stuff in the front of the car<br>-the cig. lighter<br>-and just about everthing else in the car  <p>The bigger plug on the drivers side all goes to the motor. You probably knew that but just in case...<p>
Z||000003||lowCG||07-02-99||11:08 PM||||Sounds like you've got a research project on your hands! A cool one,with some great potential outcomes;get a wiring diagram from both cars,you may wind up moving a few,or all of the wires within the plug,maybe none.<br>It can't be all that substantially different in all,just re-arranged possibly.<br>Wonder if those heads/intake flow alot better than the Pontiac iron ones? Might be a good idea to check hood clearance,not that it'll stop you or anything. <br>The extra wires could be provided for additional fuel pump control,via the airflow sensor,or some other addition that this system has.
Z||000004||batboy||07-03-99||12:06 AM||||Those aluminum splayed or canted valve heads (similar to the Chevy big block head design) are supposed to flow better and are more efficient than the cast iron ones. The combusion chamber is sort of heart shaped (GM calls it a "fast burn" chamber) and the inlet ports are redesigned to promote a swirl in the fuel mixture to spread the flame front across the chamber faster than the iron ones. The heads of the valves are unshrouded better too. I'm not completely sure how much of a power gain this translates to, but most hotrodders know that a lot of power can be made with the right heads. I stated in an earlier discussion that this engine might make a good swap. This is a 3.1 engine right?
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||07-03-99||10:24 AM||||I have seen some aluminum heads with splayed valves and heart-shaped chambers. Provisions for 1.72" intake and 1.42" exhaust valves high velocity intake runners for $565.00 a pair. Worth it?
Z||000006||batboy||07-03-99||12:17 PM||||SCCA Fiero, if you're thinking of putting just those heads onto your Fiero's V6, I believe it's more complicated than that. My understanding is (not 100% sure) that they take a different intake manifold and fuel injection setup. This means that the electronics are probably different too. Cooter is in a great situation because he has the whole donor car to work with. If you have to run around to a dozen different places and have to buy everything separately, it's probably not worth the hassle and expense. If those heads are complete and finished, ready to bolt on they might be worth that (still kind of steep in my opinion though). If they are used and need rebuilt, personally I would pass. You can probably find an entire used 3.1 engine with those heads on it for about that price if you look around. I'd do some more research if I were you.
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||07-03-99||12:49 PM||||Batboy, they are brand new GM heads,check it out.... <A HREF="http://www.maguireautomotivegroup.com/V660HEAD.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.maguireautomotivegroup.com/V660HEAD.htm</A> 
Z||000008||Eric||07-03-99||05:46 PM||||The iron heads are over $100 more than the aluminum.  It may be worth it, however. I haven't priced head work for my 3.2 rebuild yet.  I didn't think the 3.1 and 2.8 used the same valves.  I was concerned about not having big enough valves when I stroked the motor.  Now that I think about it, the 3.1 and 2.8 are exactly the same except for the cranks and pistons.  That info should save me some money.  Hopefully, if they are still good, I can retain my stock valves.  I read somewhere, the 2.8 is a destroked version of the 3.1.  And by adding a 3.1 crank, you can increase the strength of the engine because it was originally designed to be a 3.1.<p>SCCA, have you ever figured out why your V6 has so much power?  Could it be a 3.1?
Z||000009||SCCA FIERO||07-03-99||11:30 PM||||Eric, the motor runs so good I figure why mess with it. I want to rip it apart pretty bad,but I don't have time right now. I want to get it back together before July 11th so I can race again. 
Z||000010||GREENDANO||07-04-99||06:59 AM||||You guys are forgetting one important thing about the aluminum heads.  They will physically fit the 2.8 block, but the Fiero intake is not compatible.  Also, the pistons would have to be changed too.  It's not just a matter of bolting on a set of heads and you're done.  As per GM, stock cast iron headed 2.8's are capable of producing over one HP per cubic inch.  That's with iron heads and expensive deep pocket race team's wallets though.<br>  The amount of reworking that is needed to make the GenII motor/electronics work with a Fiero doesn't outweigh the benefits.  It would be like engineering a Ford 3.0 (not a SHO)out of a Taurus to fit into a Fiero because "the motor is bigger."  The outcome is not that great for the work put into it.<br>  Why do I know this?  I have a GenII 2.8 sitting in my garage from the dealership I work at.  It had a severe coolant leak coming from an imprint on the deck surface from bad machining.  The motor was removed under warranty, and after inspection it was determined to just put a new motor in for customer satisfaction.  So I have the low mile motor, and I have done the research, and I have not done the swap.  I rebuilt my 2.8 into a 3.2 instead.  That combo with a GM performance cam just about makes 200HP.  My car hauls a** now and is a much beter ride now that it has some power on tap.  It's not race car fast, that's what my pro-streeter is for, but it does go very well!<br>  That's my input.<br>     Dan-O
